<title>USA: High Plains Wheat - Kansas gets unexpected snow.</title>
<headline>High Plains Wheat - Kansas gets unexpected snow.</headline>
<dateline>KANSAS CITY, Mo. 1997-01-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Portions of eastern Kansas received several inches of snow Wednesday night, and grain dealers said the snow would likely protect the crop against bitter cold temperatures expected later Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;We weren't really expecting any snow, but (growers) are sure glad to see it,&quot; said one grain merchandiser in south central Kansas.</p>
<p>The Kansas State University Extension Office in Sumner County, which is historically one of the largest wheat producing districts in Kansas, said the county had received about three inches of snow overnight.</p>
<p>Dealers said Wichita and Hutchinson, Kan., received two to three inches of snow. Eastern and central Oklahoma also received two to three inches of snow, dealers there reported.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, basis bids for hard red winter wheat in Kansas were steady to lower. Bids in Oklahoma and Texas were steady to slightly firmer. One export firm in Galveston raised its bid late Wednesday to 69 over from 66 over.  </p>
